Profile delivery plugins.

Each plugin gets a node's profile bundle onto that node and runs its applier, and
returns the digest the node reports it now holds.

  default.py   rsync the bundle over and run the applier over ssh
  none.py      no live delivery; the node applies its profiles when it installs

Selection follows the usual plugin cascade: a module named after the node, else one
named after its group, else default. So a node or a whole group that cannot be reached
inbound is pointed at none.py by dropping a module of that name here.

Required method:

  deliver(node, hostname, bundle, timeout) -> (status: bool, message: str)

On success the message is the digest the node reported, optionally followed by further
lines: anything the applier warned about while still succeeding - a unit that would not
restart, a backup it could not find. Those lines end up in 'luna profile status' as the
reason, so an operator can see that a node applied its profiles and something is still
not as asked. Only the first line is compared against the digest.

Anything else is a failure, and a failure must never return a digest: recording one marks
the node in sync on a guess.

Tuning the fan-out

Deliveries run in parallel batches. The defaults are twenty in flight with no pause between
batches, which is deliberately conservative: unlike the BMC batches, this limit protects
the controller rather than the far end, since every child is an rsync plus an ssh of its
own.

A large cluster can override them by adding an optional section to luna.ini:

  [PROFILES]
  PROFILE_BATCH_SIZE = 25
  PROFILE_BATCH_DELAY = 2

Both are optional and so is the section. It is deliberately NOT declared in
common/constant.py: a declared section is a required one, and check_section aborts startup
on an ini that lacks it - before the logger exists to say why. Absent values simply fall
back to the defaults above.

luna.ini itself is rendered at install from trinityx-combined, so this section is not
shipped by default; an admin adds it to the running controller's copy, or it goes into the
installer template as a cross-repo pair.
